  "translation": {
    "literal": "The bright sun leans on the mountains and is gone; the Yellow River pours into the sea. Wishing to take in a thousand li of view, I climb one more storey of the tower.",
    "poetic": "The sun sinks behind the hills; the Yellow River rushes toward the sea. To see a thousand miles farther, climb one storey higher."
  },
  "notes": [
    {
      "id": "tower",
      "kind": "history",
      "title": "The Stork Tower",
      "body": "鸛雀樓 stood beside the Yellow River in the old city of Puzhou (in present-day Shanxi) and was named after the storks said to nest there. It was destroyed in the wars of the thirteenth century and rebuilt in 2002. Wang Zhihuan (688–742) is remembered for only a handful of surviving poems, and this is the most famous."
    },
    {
      "id": "parallel",
      "kind": "interpretation",
      "title": "Parallel lines and a proverb",
      "body": "Lines one and two are a matched pair (對仗): 白日 / 黃河, 依山 / 入海, 盡 / 流. Lines three and four have become a proverb. The wish to see further and the advice to climb higher are the poem's point: a wider view takes effort."
    },
    {
      "id": "rhyme",
      "kind": "rhyme",
      "title": "Rhyme: 流 · 樓",
      "body": "Lines two and four end in 流 (liú) and 樓 (lóu), which share the 尤 rhyme of the standard Tang rhyme book. Line one ends in 盡 and line three in 目, and neither rhymes, which is normal for a jueju."
    },
    {
      "id": "readings",
      "kind": "other",
      "title": "Words with more than one reading",
      "body": "更 is read gèng (\"even more\") here, not gēng (night watch). 盡 is read jìn, and 層 is read céng."
    }
  ],
